---
import Docs from "../../layouts/Docs.astro";
import BadgeDemo from "../../components/BadgeDemo";
import CodeBlock from "../../components/CodeBlock.astro";
---

<Docs
  title="Badge"
  description="Small labels for status, counts, and metadata."
>
  <h1 class="docs-title">Badge</h1>
  <p class="docs-lead">
    A small label for status, counts, and metadata. Nine tones, a wrapping chip
    size, and a render prop for links and buttons.
  </p>
  <div class="mt-8"><BadgeDemo client:load /></div>
  <section class="prose-section">
    <h2 class="section-title">Usage</h2>
    <CodeBlock
      lang="tsx"
      title="Badge usage"
      code={`import { Badge } from "@tulipfarm/sprout/badge";
import { Check } from "reicon-react/icons/Check";

<Badge variant="success">
  <Check aria-hidden="true" />
  Connected
</Badge>
<Badge variant="secondary">Draft</Badge>
<Badge variant="outline" size="chip">
  maya@example.com · accepted
</Badge>`}
    />
    <p>
      <code>variant</code> is <code>"default"</code>, <code>"success"</code>, <code
        >"warning"</code
      >, <code>"info"</code>, <code>"secondary"</code>, <code
        >"destructive"</code
      >, <code>"outline"</code>, <code>"ghost"</code>, or <code>"link"</code>.
      The default tone follows <code>--sprout-primary</code>, so it turns green
      with the TulipFarm preset.
    </p>
    <p>
      <code>size="default"</code> is a single-line pill. <code>size="chip"</code
      > grows in height and wraps long values such as email addresses or IDs. Direct
      child icons are sized to 12px. Mark an icon with <code
        >data-icon="inline-start"</code
      > or <code>"inline-end"</code> to tighten the padding on that side.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section class="prose-section" id="badge-api">
    <h2 class="section-title">Render as another element</h2>
    <CodeBlock
      lang="tsx"
      title="Badge render"
      code={`import { Badge, badgeVariants } from "@tulipfarm/sprout/badge";

<Badge variant="link" render={<Link to="/workflows" />}>
  View workflow
  <ArrowUpRight data-icon="inline-end" aria-hidden="true" />
</Badge>

<a href="/docs" className={badgeVariants({ variant: "outline" })}>
  Docs
</a>`}
    />
    <p>
      Badge renders a <code>span</code>. The Base UI <code>render</code> prop swaps
      the element for a link, a button, or your router's link. Links and buttons get
      hover tones, a focus ring, and pointer cursors. Plain spans stay static. <code
        >badgeVariants()</code
      > returns the same classes for elements you render yourself.
    </p>
    <p>
      The element gets <code>data-slot="badge"</code>, <code>data-variant</code
      >, and <code>data-size</code> for styling hooks. <code>className</code>, <code
        >title</code
      >, <code>role</code>, ARIA attributes, and <code>ref</code> pass through.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section class="prose-section">
    <h2 class="section-title">Content and overflow</h2>
    <p>
      A badge never grows wider than its container. In tight cells, wrap the
      text in a span with <code>min-w-0 truncate</code> and give the badge a <code
        >min-w-0</code
      > flex parent. Show the full value in a <code>title</code> on the cell, and
      summarize hidden values with an outline count such as <code>+2</code>.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section class="prose-section">
    <h2 class="section-title">Accessibility</h2>
    <p>
      A badge is plain text. Its words must carry the meaning; color only adds
      emphasis. Mark decorative icons <code>aria-hidden</code>. For changes that
      color or strikethrough implies, add visually hidden text such as "Adds" or
      "Removes".
    </p>
    <p>
      To announce status changes, put the badge in your own <code
        >role="status"</code
      > region. For a compact chip that needs a fuller name, use <code
        >role="img"</code
      > with an <code>aria-label</code>. Set <code>aria-invalid</code> to show an
      invalid value. Transitions last 120–180ms and stop when reduced motion is requested.
    </p>
    <p>
      Loading spinners, empty messages, retry actions, and status mapping belong
      to your application. Sprout supplies only the label.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section class="prose-section">
    <h2 class="section-title">Theme tokens</h2>
    <p>
      Badges use <code>--sprout-primary-subtle</code>, <code
        >--sprout-secondary</code
      >, <code>--sprout-success</code>, <code>--sprout-warning</code>, <code
        >--sprout-info</code
      >, <code>--sprout-danger</code>, and their <code>-subtle</code> pairs. Override
      them in light and dark modes like any other Sprout token.
    </p>
  </section>
</Docs>
